This is effectively message data accumulation. AFAIK it is possible we could receive only a partial message, which could be normal based on network traffic.

The decode could then throw, ref. [splitMessage](https://github.com/smithy-lang/smithy-typescript/blob/main/packages/eventstream-codec/src/splitMessage.ts) called by [EventStreamCodec](https://github.com/smithy-lang/smithy-typescript/blob/main/packages/eventstream-codec/src/EventStreamCodec.ts) or [HeaderMarshaller.parse](https://github.com/smithy-lang/smithy-typescript/blob/main/packages/eventstream-codec/src/HeaderMarshaller.ts#L86).

It has a bit of a code smell to it, admittedly. Preferable would perhaps be to do some top level frame scrutiny and gracefully continue, vs try-decode, catch-and-repeat. However, `splitMessage` is basically doing that for us, so we'd be duplicating logic (thus, introducing some fragility re: bifurcation from library) if we did it here:

```
  if (byteLength < MINIMUM_MESSAGE_LENGTH) {
    throw new Error("Provided message too short to accommodate event stream message overhead");
  }

  const view = new DataView(buffer, byteOffset, byteLength);

  const messageLength = view.getUint32(0, false);

  if (byteLength !== messageLength) {
    throw new Error("Reported message length does not match received message length");
  }
```

