### Analysis
## Sequential fetch execution in code interpreter download endpoints defeats parallel optimization

**What fails\:** `chat-openai-code-interpreter-download-files/[container]/[file]/route.ts` and `chat-azure-code-interpreter-download-files/[container]/[file]/route.ts` use `await fetch()` before `Promise.all()`\, causing sequential execution instead of parallel fetches

**How to reproduce\:**

```javascript
// Original code pattern (sequential - takes ~2000ms for two 1s requests)
const infoPromise = await fetch(url1);
const downloadPromise = await fetch(url2);
const [info, download] = await Promise.all([infoPromise, downloadPromise]);

// Fixed code pattern (parallel - takes ~1000ms for two 1s requests)
const infoPromise = fetch(url1);
const downloadPromise = fetch(url2);
const [info, download] = await Promise.all([infoPromise, downloadPromise]);
```

**Result\:** Two API calls execute sequentially\, each waiting for the previous to complete\. For requests taking 500ms each\, total time is \~1000ms instead of \~500ms\.

**Expected\:** Both fetches should start immediately and execute concurrently\. `Promise.all()` should receive pending promises\, not already\-resolved Response objects\. Per [MDN Promise\.all\(\) documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all)\, the pattern should avoid \"over\-awaiting\" code by starting promises concurrently before awaiting them together\.

</details>
