const chunk = {
  retrievedContext: {
    uri: 'https://example.com/article',
    title: undefined,  // No explicit title provided
    text: 'Some content'
  }
};

// Before the bug fix (bba012075):
// Generated source would have: title: undefined

// After the bug (commit bba012075):
// Generated source has: title: 'Unknown Document'
```

**Result\:** URL sources from retrievedContext with missing titles now return `'Unknown Document'` instead of `undefined`\, which violates the original design where URL sources \(both web and retrievedContext\) would have optional undefined titles\.

**Expected behavior\:** URL sources should maintain consistent title handling across all sources\. Web URL sources use `?? undefined`\, so retrievedContext URL sources should also use `?? undefined`\. Only document\-type sources \(with file paths or fileSearchStore\) should use `?? 'Unknown Document'` as a fallback since the document source type requires a title field per the [LanguageModelV3Source type definition](https://vercel.com/packages/provider/src/language-model/v3/language-model-v3-source.ts)\.

**Root cause\:** Commit bba012075 refactored the code to handle File Search API\'s `fileSearchStore` field\, but unintentionally changed the title handling\. The original code \(commit 0b92881ad\) had per\-source\-type title defaults\:

*   URL sources\: `title: chunk.retrievedContext.title ?? undefined`
*   Document sources\: `title: chunk.retrievedContext.title ?? 'Unknown Document'`

The refactored code consolidated this to a single line before branching\:

*   All sources\: `const title = chunk.retrievedContext.title ?? 'Unknown Document'`

This is inconsistent and breaks backward compatibility for code that checks `if (source.title === undefined)`\.

